"""Year summaries, 1926-1950. Curated narrative context for each year (what happened, why markets and the economy moved, and what it set up next).
Edited by hand from widely documented history; verify specifics against primary sources before relying on them. Figures are approximate."""
S = {
1926: "The US economy was in the middle of the 1920s expansion, driven by mass-produced automobiles, electrification, radio and an explosion of consumer instalment credit. The Florida land boom, which had drawn speculators with tiny down payments, collapsed in 1926 after a September hurricane in Miami and tighter credit, a preview of how leveraged property speculation unwinds. Yet equities were barely affected: the stock market kept grinding higher on strong corporate earnings, and broker lending was still modest compared with what came later. Prices were stable, unemployment low, and the Federal Reserve, only twelve years old, saw little reason to lean against rising asset prices. The year matters mainly as the calm before the credit-fuelled phase of the decade.",
1927: "Speculation began to build in earnest. At a July conference on Long Island, Federal Reserve Bank of New York governor Benjamin Strong and European central bankers agreed to ease credit, partly to help Britain defend the gold standard parity it had restored in 1925. The Fed cut its discount rate and bought government securities, and the extra liquidity flowed into the stock market rather than into goods and wages. Charles Lindbergh's solo Atlantic crossing in May fired public enthusiasm for aviation and every new technology. Industrial production dipped briefly in a mild recession but the market shrugged it off and rose strongly. Later critics, including some Fed officials, regarded 1927 as the moment easy money planted the seeds of the 1929 crash.",
1928: "The bull market turned into a mania. Brokers' loans, which let investors buy stock with as little as 10 percent down, climbed past $4 billion, and call money rates spiked as margin borrowers competed for funds. Radio Corporation of America, utilities and other 'new era' stocks soared on stories rather than profits, and investment trusts multiplied. The Federal Reserve began raising rates in 1928 and warned member banks about speculative lending, but corporations and foreign lenders kept pouring money into the call-loan market, so tightening had little effect on prices. Herbert Hoover was elected on a promise of continued prosperity. Gold flowed to the US, squeezing Europe and foreshadowing the global strain that would transmit the coming slump.",
1929: "The year of the great crash. The Dow Jones Industrial Average peaked at about 381 on 3 September after a final speculative surge fed by margin debt. A sharp fall in October culminated in Black Thursday on 24 October and Black Tuesday on 29 October, when record volume overwhelmed the ticker and the market lost roughly a fifth of its value in two sessions. Bankers' attempts to prop up prices failed. By year end the Dow was down about a third, yet many believed the worst was over. The crash destroyed wealth, cut consumption and investment, exposed overleveraged investment trusts and banks, and marked the start of the slide that would take the Dow down roughly 89 percent by July 1932.",
1930: "A partial recovery in early 1930 convinced many that the crash was a financial correction, but the real economy was weakening fast. Industrial output, prices and farm incomes fell, and unemployment climbed past 8 percent by year end. Congress passed the Smoot-Hawley Tariff in June, raising duties on thousands of imports; trading partners retaliated and world trade began to contract. In November and December the first major wave of US bank failures hit, including the Bank of United States in New York, which wiped out depositors and eroded confidence. The Federal Reserve let the money supply shrink rather than act as lender of last resort. The market gave back its spring rally and set new lows, confirming a deepening depression.",
1931: "A banking crisis became global. In May the Credit-Anstalt, Austria's largest bank, collapsed, and panic spread through Central Europe as depositors withdrew funds and capital fled to gold. Germany imposed exchange controls, and Hoover proposed a one-year moratorium on war debts in June, but it came too late to stop contagion. In September Britain suspended the gold standard and the pound fell sharply, forcing a scramble among other countries. The US Fed responded by raising rates to defend gold, which tightened credit as banks were failing. Unemployment approached 16 percent, thousands of banks closed, and stocks fell again, with the Dow losing about half of its value over the year. The episode showed how rigid gold-standard commitments transmitted deflation across borders.",
1932: "The trough of the Great Depression. The Dow bottomed on 8 July at roughly 41, almost 90 percent below its 1929 peak, and unemployment neared 24 percent. Output was about a quarter below its 1929 level, thousands of banks had failed, and deflation raised the real burden of debts. Congress created the Reconstruction Finance Corporation in January to lend to banks and railroads and passed the Glass-Steagall Act easing credit, while the Revenue Act raised taxes, deepening the squeeze. The Bonus Army marched on Washington. Franklin Roosevelt won the November election on a promise of a New Deal. Markets rallied sharply from the summer low, and for investors the year marks the point of maximum pessimism.",
1933: "Roosevelt took office in March amid a nationwide banking panic and declared a bank holiday within days, followed by the Emergency Banking Act, which allowed only sound banks to reopen. Deposits flowed back and stocks surged on the news. Congress passed the Glass-Steagall Act, creating federal deposit insurance and separating commercial from investment banking, and the Securities Act, the first federal disclosure law for new issues. In April the US left the gold standard, and the dollar fell, ending deflation and lifting commodity prices. The Dow gained roughly 60 percent in the calendar year from depressed levels, one of the strongest annual rebounds ever. The recovery was real but fragile: unemployment remained above 20 percent.",
1934: "Reform and recovery continued. The Securities Exchange Act created the SEC, with Joseph Kennedy as first chair, to police markets, require ongoing disclosure and curb manipulation. The Gold Reserve Act devalued the dollar by fixing gold at $35 an ounce and gave the Treasury title to the Fed's gold, a reflationary step that raised the dollar price of gold by nearly 70 percent. Economic output kept growing and unemployment edged down, though still very high. Equity markets made only modest net gains after the previous year's surge, with investors wary of regulatory changes. The Dust Bowl devastated farm regions, and the New Deal's agricultural and industrial programmes were under legal challenge. The year consolidated the architecture of modern financial regulation.",
1935: "The economy recovered steadily while political controversy over the New Deal intensified. Congress passed the Social Security Act in August, creating old-age pensions and unemployment insurance, and the Banking Act of 1935 reshaped the Federal Reserve by concentrating open-market authority in a Board-influenced committee and strengthening central control. The Supreme Court struck down the National Industrial Recovery Act in the Schechter decision, undoing much of the early New Deal industrial codes. Stocks rose strongly, and industrial production and corporate profits improved, but unemployment was still above 18 percent. The year also saw the Wagner Act protecting union organising and the establishment of public works agencies that hired millions.",
1936: "The recovery strengthened, with output growing at a rapid pace and unemployment falling toward 14 percent. Equities rose through the year and the Dow ended near 180, recovering well over half of its post-crash losses. Roosevelt won a landslide reelection in November. Late in the year the Fed became concerned about excess bank reserves that could fuel inflation and began tightening policy, raising reserve requirements in August and again later, a decision that would prove costly. Veterans' bonus payments boosted spending. The Spanish Civil War and the militarisation of Germany heightened geopolitical risk, and gold continued to flow into the US as Europe feared war, supporting the dollar and Treasury holdings.",
1937: "A policy-induced recession. In 1936 and early 1937 the Fed doubled bank reserve requirements, and the Treasury began sterilising gold inflows, while fiscal policy tightened as Social Security payroll taxes began and relief spending was cut. Credit conditions tightened and the economy turned down sharply from mid-year, with industrial production falling by about a third and unemployment climbing back toward 19 percent. The stock market peaked in March and then fell roughly half by 1938, one of the sharpest bear markets on record. The 'Roosevelt recession' showed that withdrawing support too early could undo a recovery and is often cited as a lesson in premature tightening.",
1938: "The downturn bottomed in the spring. The Dow reached its trough in March and the economy began recovering after the government reversed course: the Fed eased reserve requirements, the Treasury ended sterilisation of gold, and Congress approved new spending on relief and public works. Roosevelt also signed the Fair Labor Standards Act, setting a minimum wage and maximum hours. The market rebounded strongly from its lows and finished the year well above them. Abroad, Germany annexed Austria in March and the Munich Agreement in September increased fears of war, while European capital kept moving to the US. The year illustrates how quickly renewed stimulus can restore growth after a policy mistake.",
1939: "World War II began in September when Germany invaded Poland. Equities initially jumped on the prospect of war orders, with commodity prices and defence stocks leading, but the rally faded as the conflict dragged on and uncertainty rose. The US remained neutral but repealed its arms embargo through the cash-and-carry provisions, boosting demand for American goods. Unemployment was still near 17 percent, and the economy had not fully regained its 1929 output. Gold continued to flow into the US as foreign investors sought safety, and interest rates stayed very low. The year set the stage for the wartime industrial mobilisation that would finally end the Depression.",
1940: "Markets were hit in May when Germany invaded the Low Countries and France, which fell by June. The Dow dropped sharply on the news before stabilising, and Britain's survival in the Battle of Britain restored some confidence. The US began a major rearmament drive, with Congress approving large defence appropriations, the first peacetime draft, and the Lend-Lease concept being developed. Unemployment fell below 15 percent for the first time in years as defence orders increased factory output. Roosevelt won an unprecedented third term in November. Interest rates stayed at historic lows, and capital kept flowing into the US from war-torn Europe.",
1941: "The year ended with the attack on Pearl Harbor on 7 December, which brought the US into the war. Before that, the economy was already mobilising: Lend-Lease aid to Britain was enacted in March, unemployment fell below 10 percent, and industrial production rose strongly. Inflation began to build, leading to the creation of the Office of Price Administration. Stocks drifted lower through the year as taxes rose, price controls loomed and war risk mounted, and fell further after Pearl Harbor. The Treasury began selling war bonds. The shift to a command-style war economy would determine the shape of markets for the next four years, with rationing, controlled prices and fixed low interest rates.",
1942: "The market found its low in April, about when the Allies' fortunes began turning, and the long wartime bull market started. The economy converted fully to war production, with output growing at record rates, unemployment dropping below 5 percent and corporate profits strong despite high taxes and wage and price controls. The Fed agreed to peg Treasury bill and bond yields at low levels to help finance the war cheaply, a policy that lasted until 1951. Rationing of fuel, rubber and food began. Battles at Midway, Stalingrad and El Alamein shifted momentum. Equities, cheap by historical standards, began a steady rise that continued until the war's end.",
1943: "The war economy was in full flow. Unemployment fell to about 2 percent, industrial output was at record highs and the government ran very large deficits financed by war bond drives and bank credit, with the Fed holding interest rates low. Price controls and rationing suppressed inflation, but household savings built up as consumers had little to buy, creating pent-up demand for after the war. Stocks gained steadily on strong earnings and the improving military outlook following the Allied invasion of Italy. Investors worried about what would happen when the war ended, with many economists predicting a return to depression, a fear that proved unfounded.",
1944: "The Bretton Woods conference in July set the framework of the postwar monetary order, creating the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank and pegging currencies to a gold-convertible US dollar at $35 an ounce. The Allied invasion of Normandy on 6 June opened the western front, and stocks rose on the growing expectation of victory and the economic rotation to peacetime. The government continued deficit financing at fixed low rates, and the economy ran near full capacity. The GI Bill was signed in June, providing education and housing support for returning veterans and shaping the postwar boom. Roosevelt won a fourth term in November.",
1945: "The war ended: Germany surrendered in May and Japan in August after the atomic bombings. Roosevelt died in April and Harry Truman became president. Stocks rose through the year as investors concluded that demobilisation would not bring a new depression, and the Dow gained about a third. The Bretton Woods agreements took effect in December, formally establishing the IMF and World Bank. Reconversion brought temporary job losses and strikes, but consumer savings and pent-up demand supported the transition. The US emerged as the dominant economic power, producing about half of the world's manufactured goods. Price controls remained in place, but pressure to lift them was building.",
1946: "Reconversion produced inflation and labour unrest. Price controls were largely lifted in mid-year, and consumer prices jumped by more than 8 percent, while a wave of strikes hit steel, autos, coal and railroads. The stock market peaked in May, then fell sharply in the autumn as investors worried about inflation and a postwar recession that did not arrive. The baby boom began as returning soldiers started families, driving demand for housing, cars and appliances. The Employment Act established the goal of maximum employment. The year showed how pent-up demand and the end of controls can produce sharp price spikes even as the economy transitions to peacetime.",
1947: "The Cold War took shape. In March the Truman Doctrine pledged support for countries resisting communism, starting with Greece and Turkey, and in June Secretary of State George Marshall announced the plan that would send billions of dollars to rebuild Western Europe, supporting demand for American exports. Inflation eased after the previous year's jump and stocks moved sideways, with valuations low relative to earnings and dividend yields high. Congress passed the Taft-Hartley Act over Truman's veto, restricting union powers. The postwar housing and consumer boom continued. Investors remained cautious, expecting a recession, and equities offered high yields compared with bonds.",
1948: "Tensions with the Soviet Union escalated with the Berlin blockade starting in June and the airlift that followed. The Marshall Plan began disbursing aid, rebuilding trade partners. The US economy expanded for most of the year but slipped into a mild recession late in the year as inventories were cut and the Fed's tightening of credit took effect. Truman won a surprise reelection in November. Stocks were range-bound, held back by recession fears and uncertainty but supported by low valuations and strong earnings. The dollar and gold stayed tied under the new Bretton Woods order, and the early signs of Europe's industrial recovery appeared.",
1949: "A mild recession ran through the first part of the year, and the stock market made its low in June, starting a bull market that would last, with interruptions, until the mid-1960s. Equities were cheap, yielding more than bonds, and corporate earnings recovered as the economy turned up in the fourth quarter. Britain devalued sterling by 30 percent in September, and many other currencies followed, adjusting the postwar exchange rate structure. The Soviet Union tested an atomic bomb, the communists won the Chinese civil war, and NATO was formed. Investor sentiment was still depressed, a classic pattern in which a durable bull market begins when pessimism is widespread.",
1950: "The Korean War began on 25 June when North Korea invaded the South, and stocks fell sharply for several weeks before recovering and ending the year higher. Defence spending, stockpiling and fears of shortages pushed commodity prices and inflation up, and the government reintroduced price and wage controls early in 1951. The economy grew strongly, with unemployment falling below 5 percent, and corporate profits surged. The Federal Reserve was increasingly uncomfortable with its commitment to peg bond yields in the face of rising inflation, a conflict with the Treasury that would be resolved the following year. The year confirmed that geopolitical shocks often produce short-lived market declines when the economic backdrop is strong.",
}
